<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable Settings view-models (Phase 3 · W7 · Settings lane, parity-100 wave).

    This is the Windows peer of the per-tab macOS Settings view state:
      AgentLens/Views/Settings/{ModelProxy,AlertsAndNotifications,TextExpansion,
        Daemon,Account,CloudStore,DevicesAndSync}SettingsView.swift,
      AgentLens/Views/ComputerUse/ComputerUseSettingsView.swift,
      AgentLens/Views/Settings/SettingsView.swift (inline PetCompanionSettingsView),
      AgentLens/Views/Settings/AgentsSettingsView.swift,
      and their backing stores under AgentLens/Services/Settings/Stores/.

    It targets plain net8.0 — NO Windows TFM, zero P/Invoke, zero UI framework —
    on purpose: the SAME managed assembly the WinUI shell (net8.0-windows) links is
    unit-tested on the macOS authoring host today (`dotnet test`). Mirrors the
    already-proven OpenBurnBar.App.Settings / OpenBurnBar.App.Presentation portable
    pattern: a higher-runtime test project (net10.0) references this net8.0 library,
    and the WinUI app (net8.0-windows) references the very same library.

    Everything OS/data-bound is behind an injectable seam (an interface declared
    here); tests wire in-memory fakes, the WinUI shell wires the real Windows core.
    The macOS settings model (state + actions + validation) is reproduced faithfully;
    the WinUI XAML leaf pages that x:Bind these view-models are bucket-B / dev-host-
    deferred at the XamlCompiler gate.

    Feature cores backing these view-models (all portable net8.0, on main):
      • windows/computeruse/OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Core   (Computer Use trust/mode enums)
      • windows/app/OpenBurnBar.App.TextExpansion           (snippet model + trigger validator)
      • windows/app/OpenBurnBar.App.ManagedAgentRuntime     (#1301 Hermes/Pi runtime status)
      • windows/app/OpenBurnBar.App.CursorConnector         (#1303 Cursor/model-proxy config)
      • windows/app/OpenBurnBar.App.Presentation            (quota snapshot + Budget domain)
      • windows/app/OpenBurnBar.App.Settings                (SettingsTab + AgentProvider identity)

    Account / Cloud / DevicesAndSync are data-gated: their live sign-in state comes
    from the OAuth credential gate (#1304 DesktopOAuthCredentialsProvider.IsSignedIn /
    FirebaseOAuthSession). This project models the seam (IAccountSessionGate etc.);
    the WinUI host wires the real provider. Tests drive a fake signed-in state.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.App.Settings.ViewModels</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.App.Settings.ViewModels</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      Lets the net8.0 library be RESTORED/BUILT by the net10.0 SDK on macOS without
      a Windows targeting pack. Pure managed library, so no Windows facet is needed.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenBurnBar.App.Settings\OpenBurnBar.App.Settings.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenBurnBar.App.Presentation\OpenBurnBar.App.Presentation.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenBurnBar.App.TextExpansion\OpenBurnBar.App.TextExpansion.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenBurnBar.App.ManagedAgentRuntime\OpenBurnBar.App.ManagedAgentRuntime.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenBurnBar.App.CursorConnector\OpenBurnBar.App.CursorConnector.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\computeruse\OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Core\OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
